{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-38182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\n- $Q$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- $Q$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(Q)=-5$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-38182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}|+6|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-3(8-14)+6(5-8) \\\\  & =0-3\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18-18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-38182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8896951a70b3c68de4b197a716eaaa1e89a0ab050bf47fc124b4a06e30124bfb"} {"question" "q-38182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-38182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-38182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-69835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\n- $T$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(T)=-3$\n- $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-69835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(2+3)-2(1+6)-1(-1+4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 7-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =20-14-3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=3\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-69835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8896951a70b3c68de4b197a716eaaa1e89a0ab050bf47fc124b4a06e30124bfb"} {"question" "q-69835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-69835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-69835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $det(A)=37$, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}x & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}3 & x \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(x\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 5)-(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2(x-20)-(3+8) \\\\  & =2x-40-11 \\\\  & =2x-51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $37,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-51 & =37 \\\\ 2x & =88 \\\\ x & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8896951a70b3c68de4b197a716eaaa1e89a0ab050bf47fc124b4a06e30124bfb"} {"question" "q-280403" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280403" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "44"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280519" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $G=[\\begin{aligned}x & 2x-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & x+1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ is singular, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280519" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det & =|\\begin{aligned}x & 2x-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & x+1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ x+1 & -4\\end{aligned}|-(2x-1)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|+6|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & x+1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x(3\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (x+1))-(2x-1)(0)+6(0\\cdot (x+1)-3\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =x(-12)+0+6(3) \\\\  & =-12x+18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+18 & =0 \\\\ -12x & =-18 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280519" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8896951a70b3c68de4b197a716eaaa1e89a0ab050bf47fc124b4a06e30124bfb"} {"question" "q-280519" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280519" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the determinant of $[\\begin{aligned}x & 2x & 5 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is $40$, find all the possible values of $x$ **in ascending order**.\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let’s use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}x & 2x & 5 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x|\\begin{aligned}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2x|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}|+5|\\begin{aligned}3 & x \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 7)+5(3-7x) \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(-22)+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+44x+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+5x+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $40$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+5x+15 & =40 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+5x-25 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}-4(2)(-25)}}{2(2)} \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5+\\sqrt{225}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, **in ascending order**, the solutions are $x=-5$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8896951a70b3c68de4b197a716eaaa1e89a0ab050bf47fc124b4a06e30124bfb"} {"question" "q-280541" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280541" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280541" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"} {"question" "q-280541" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7d2588b7169b19a61e60e3f6f26d32b11bc51681a14859a1af9262cd6955380"}] "usage" {} "basis" 867 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
